        "original_id": "35588fbb-dfe0-5430-970d-fa4ee5e478e3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Michael Wadleigh, Barbara Kopple, Cathy Caplan, Thomas Haneke, Lawrence Silk\n- Films: Woodstock, American Dream, Harlan County, U.S.A.\n- Events: Woodstock Festival, Hormel Foods strike\n- Places: Bethel, New York, Austin, Minnesota, Akron, Ohio\n\nFacts:\n- The Woodstock Festival took place in August 1969 near Bethel, New York.\n- Michael Wadleigh was born on September 24, 1942 in Akron, Ohio.\n- Woodstock is a 1970 documentary film about the 1969 Woodstock Festival.\n- Michael Wadleigh is an American film director and cinematographer renowned for his documentary film Woodstock.\n- American Dream is a 1990 cinéma vérité documentary film.\n- Barbara Kopple directed American Dream with co-directors Cathy Caplan, Thomas Haneke, and Lawrence Silk.\n- Harlan County, U.S.A. was directed and produced by Barbara Kopple.\n- Harlan County, U.S.A. is less ambivalent toward unions than Barbara Kopple's later film American Dream.\n- American Dream recounts the 1985–86 Hormel Foods strike in Austin, Minnesota.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "1f88fb2e-b1d1-597b-a1f2-d053d25d63cd",
        "text": "Page: Woodstock (film). Sentence: Woodstock is a 1970 documentary film of the watershed counterculture Woodstock Festival which took place in August 1969 near Bethel, New York. \"\nPage: American Dream (film). Sentence: American Dream is a 1990 \"cinéma vérité\" documentary film directed by Barbara Kopple and co-directed by Cathy Caplan, Thomas Haneke, and Lawrence Silk.\nPage: Michael Wadleigh. Sentence: Michael Wadleigh, also known as Michael Wadley (born September 24, 1942 in Akron, Ohio), is an American film director and cinematographer renowned for his groundbreaking documentary of the 1969 Woodstock Festival, \"Woodstock\".\nPage: Harlan County, USA. Sentence: Directed and produced by Barbara Kopple, who has long been an advocate of workers' rights, \"Harlan County, U.S.A.\" is less ambivalent in its attitude toward unions than her later \"American Dream\", the account of the Hormel Foods strike in Austin, Minnesota in 1985-86.",
